<h2>The allegations</h2>
<p>The FIR was registered on the complaint of a political party&rsquo;s Delhi unit president, alleging that the applicant posted a video on social media — with a substantial view count by the time of registration — containing caste-based abuse directed at a sitting Member of Parliament and remarks concerning Dr B.R. Ambedkar and the Scheduled Caste and Scheduled Tribe communities. The FIR invoked Sections 3(2)(va), 3(1)(r), 3(1)(s), 3(1)(u), 3(1)(v) and 3(1)(w) of the SC/ST Act, Section 67 of the IT Act and Sections 196(1)(c) and 351(3) of the BNS.</p>

<h2>The legal gate: Sections 18 and 18A</h2>
<p>Section 18 of the SC/ST Act excludes the application of the anticipatory bail provision to offences under the Act; Section 18A(2), inserted in 2018, reinforces that exclusion notwithstanding any judgment, direction or order. The Supreme Court&rsquo;s decisions — including the 2024 ruling in <em>Shajan Skaria</em> and the 2025 ruling in <em>Kiran v. Rajkumar Jivraj Jain</em>, both relied upon in this judgment — preserve a single, narrow exception: if, on a prima facie reading, the FIR discloses no offence under the Act, the bar does not bite.</p>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Step 1.</strong> Read the FIR and accompanying material on its face — no mini-trial, no weighing of defences.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Step 2.</strong> If no SC/ST Act offence is prima facie disclosed, the anticipatory bail court can proceed on merits.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Step 3.</strong> If any such offence is prima facie made out, the application fails as not maintainable — the merits are never reached.</div>
</div>

<h2>How the test was applied</h2>
<p>The court did not stop at the FIR&rsquo;s recitals: it viewed the video in chambers and read the transcript. Two concessions framed the analysis — the applicant is not a member of a Scheduled Caste or Tribe, and the video was published within public view. On the content, the court found the statements to be explicit caste references rather than stray or ambiguous remarks, holding that offences under Sections 3(1)(r) and 3(1)(s) were prima facie made out. The plea that the remarks were a reaction to provocation failed: the provocation was not attributed to the complainant&rsquo;s side, and there had been no apology or retraction. Having found two clauses prima facie attracted, the court did not separately examine the remaining provisions — the bar had already closed the door.</p>

<h2>What practitioners should take from it</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>The threshold is the whole battle.</strong> In SC/ST Act matters, anticipatory bail succeeds or fails on the prima facie question alone. Arguments about evidence quality, motive or eventual acquittal belong to the trial or to regular bail, not here.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Context is examined, but narrowly.</strong> Courts may look at the actual material — here, the video itself — to see whether the words used carry the statutory ingredients, such as intentional insult within public view. That examination is not an invitation to argue defences.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Online speech is squarely covered.</strong> A social media post visible to the public satisfies the &ldquo;public view&rdquo; element. The medium gives no immunity; the view count only underlines publicity.</div>
</div>
<p>The decision sits alongside the established position that where the bar operates, the remedy lies in the ordinary criminal process — cooperation with the investigation and, if arrested, a regular bail application in which the merits receive full consideration. For anyone facing an SC/ST Act allegation in Delhi, the practical lesson is to assess the FIR&rsquo;s ingredients clause by clause before investing in a pre-arrest strategy the statute may not permit.</p>
